import sys
p = sys.argv[1]; s = open(p, encoding="utf-8").read()
R = [
(r"and compare it with the same intervention on role 2 and with three edits of the same norm: a random direction, a random direction inside the range of $B_1$, and the natural direction $h(a', b') - h(a, b)$, which changes both parts.",
 r"and compare it with the same intervention on role 2 and with five edits of the same norm: a random direction; a random direction inside the range of $B_1$ with isotropic coefficients; a direction inside the range of $B_1$ with coefficients drawn from the code distribution, $B_1(e(a_3) - e(a_4))$ for two fresh glyphs; the natural direction $h(a', b') - h(a, b)$, which changes both parts; and the network's own change when only the role-1 glyph changes, $h(a', b) - h(a, b)$."),
(r"Two controls separate ordinary forgetting from the role structure: the same fine-tuning on a training glyph, and fine-tuning on the fresh glyph in both roles.",
 r"Two controls separate ordinary forgetting from the role structure: the same fine-tuning on a training glyph, and fine-tuning on the fresh glyph in both roles. A last condition uses no gradient step: it reads the fresh glyph's code from one composite in which it occupies role 1 and writes it into role 2 through $B_2$, which leaves old pairs untouched by construction."),
(r"its spill is 0.16. The intervention on role 2 is equally local (spill 0.20). Edits of the same norm that do not follow a role's code are not: the spill is 0.90 for the natural direction, 0.75 for a random direction inside the range of $B_1$, and 0.96 for a random direction (Figure~\ref{fig:structure}c). Locality is therefore a property of the role components that the readouts identify, not of any edit in the range of $B_1$. At $V = 16$ the spill of the role-1 intervention is larger (0.35, or 0.28 without the transformer) but still well below the natural direction (0.85).",
 r"its spill is 0.16. The intervention on role 2 is nearly as local (spill 0.21), and both are as local as the network's own change when only the role-1 glyph changes (0.25). Edits that do not follow the code distribution are not: the spill is 0.90 for the natural direction, which changes both glyphs, 0.95 for a random direction, and 0.75 for a random direction inside the range of $B_1$ with isotropic coefficients, against 0.30 when the coefficients follow the code distribution (Figure~\ref{fig:structure}c). Locality is therefore a property of role-1 components of the kind the network encodes, not of every direction in the range of $B_1$: the decoder respects the role code on the distribution of codes it was trained on. At $V = 16$ every edit spills more, the role-1 intervention 0.36 against 0.43 for the network's own role-1 change and 0.85 for the natural direction."),
(r"and the interventions spill more (0.32 at $V = 64$ and 0.61 at $V = 16$, against 1.06 and 1.15 for the natural direction).",
 r"and the interventions spill more (0.31 at $V = 64$ and 0.61 at $V = 16$, against 0.54 and 0.87 for the network's own role-1 change and 1.06 and 1.14 for the natural direction)."),
(r"Fine-tuned on it in role 1 alone, without replay, they forget held-out pairs (0.98 to 0.91), and they render it in role 2 \emph{worse} than before (0.63), although its role-1 rendering improves to 0.92. Part of the forgetting is ordinary: the same fine-tuning on a training glyph costs held-out pairs 0.04 (to 0.94), against 0.07 for the fresh glyph. The damage to role 2 is specific to learning in one role: fine-tuning on the fresh glyph in both roles, with the same number of composites, renders it at 0.89 and 0.91. With 16 training glyphs the same pattern is larger (held-out pairs 0.97 to 0.63 against 0.85 for a training glyph). Replay of training composites prevents both the forgetting and the damage to role 2 (0.98 and 0.77). A role-factorized state permits adding a part by adding its code; fine-tuning on one role does not exploit that structure.",
 r"Fine-tuned on it in role 1 alone, without replay, they forget held-out pairs (0.98 to 0.90), and they render it in role 2 \emph{worse} than before (0.62), although its role-1 rendering improves to 0.92. Paired within runs, the role-2 rendering falls by $0.13 \pm 0.02$ and held-out pairs by $0.08 \pm 0.01$, an excess of $0.05 \pm 0.02$ (mean $\pm$ standard error over 40 runs). The excess is not specific to new parts: the same fine-tuning on a training glyph lowers that glyph's role-2 rendering from 0.95 to 0.87 and held-out pairs to 0.94, an excess of $0.04 \pm 0.01$. Fine-tuning in one role thus damages the other role more than it damages old pairs, whatever the glyph; fine-tuning on the fresh glyph in both roles, with the same number of composites, renders it at 0.89 and 0.91. With 16 training glyphs the pattern is larger and now specific to the fresh glyph (held-out pairs 0.97 to 0.63, against 0.85 for a training glyph; excess $0.22 \pm 0.06$ against $0.04 \pm 0.02$). Replay of training composites prevents both the forgetting and the damage to role 2 (0.98 and 0.77). Writing the fresh glyph's code into role 2 without any gradient step renders it exactly as well as the untouched network does (0.75 against 0.75 at $V = 64$) and better than fine-tuning (0.62), with no cost to old pairs; at $V = 16$, where the decoder cannot render fresh glyphs at all, it does not help ($-0.17$ against $-0.13$). A role-factorized state permits adding a part by adding its code; fine-tuning on one role does not exploit that structure, and the code alone helps only as far as the decoder can render it."),
(r"for interventions on role 1 and role 2 and for edits of the same norm along the natural direction $h(a', b') - h(a, b)$, a random direction inside the range of $B_1$, and a random direction ($V \in \{16, 64\}$).",
 r"for interventions on role 1 and role 2 and for edits of the same norm: the network's own change when only the role-1 glyph changes, a direction in the range of $B_1$ with coefficients from the code distribution, the natural direction $h(a', b') - h(a, b)$, a random direction in the range of $B_1$, and a random direction ($V \in \{16, 64\}$)."),
(r"in one role with replay, in both roles, and, as a control, on a training glyph in one role (\textsc{over}, $V = 64$).",
 r"in one role with replay, and in both roles, and after writing its code into role 2 without fine-tuning (\textsc{over}, $V = 64$); the training-glyph control is reported in the text."),
(r"replacing one role's component with that of another composite changes the output for that role and leaves the other nearly untouched (spill 0.16 against 0.90 for a natural change of the same size; Figure~\ref{fig:structure}c). The separation holds for components taken from real composites; a random direction within the same role subspace spills (0.75), so the decoder respects the role code only on the distribution of codes it was trained on.",
 r"replacing one role's component with that of another composite changes the output for that role and leaves the other nearly untouched, as locally as the network's own response to a change of that glyph (spill 0.16 against 0.25; Figure~\ref{fig:structure}c). The separation holds for components drawn from the code distribution (0.30) and fails for isotropic directions in the same subspace (0.75), so the decoder respects the role code only on the distribution of codes it was trained on."),
]
for a, b in R:
    assert s.count(a) == 1, a[:80]
    s = s.replace(a, b)
open(p, "w", encoding="utf-8").write(s); print("ok")
